00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.210 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back Boris Johnson says he's  getting tough on law and order and  failing a wave of new policies including  more prison places and an extension of  stop and search powers with me is Kip  Malthouse the new Minister for policing  and crime a longtime colleague of the  new PM thank you very much so let's  start first of all perhaps you've seen  it Michael Heseltine Betty Boothroyd  they've written an op-ed in The Sunday  Times talking about Sir no deal brexit  being the most grotesque act of national  self-harm committed in peacetime by  British government it's a big accusation  particular coming from a toy owner of  your policy yeah I mean obviously  there's a lot of hyperbole being thrown  about as we approach the date of the  31st of October but in the end even  people like Lord Hesseltine great  figures from the past who have never  quite reconciled themselves the idea  that we're going to leave the European  Union are gonna have to focus on the  fact that that's what the British people  commanded us to do and that's what the  government is committing to do at the  end of October do you think we're ready  October 31st I think we will be ready  yes I mean obviously there's a huge  amount of work to be done between now  and then you know the cabinet committees  were meeting on a daily basis you know  Michael Gove is leading the charge he's  been in Northern Ireland recently told  them about preparations there the Prime  Minister has made it very clear that the  Treasury has to put money forward to  help with those preparations and also to  be ready to ameliorate it any impact  that may be well if we do leave with no  deal alongside that obviously profoundly  the government is trying to get a deal  and we want the Europeans to recognize  that a deal is in both of our interests  that they need to start talking to us  about what that might look like and  specifically on policing I mean let's  let's look there's there was a document  that was leaked to my colleague Sam  Coates not that long ago about the  consequences in the first day the first  fortnight the first month of an Oda  brexit and it's got some pretty stark  conclusions about what might well happen  actually we've got the document right  here and particularly in that middle  column security possible in the first  fortnight possible increased risk of  serious organised crime people smuggling  illegal migration and then look at the  far-right heightened policing resource  over the first month unsustainable  operational gaps continue to emerge this  is kind of scary stuff it's very yo I'm  not going to comment on leaked  government documents but it is it is  fair to say that the police like every  other part of the government  be prepared for No Deal and we need to  make sure that those forces across the  land are doing what they need to do to  make sure that they can continue to  function in the event of no deal now  there are lots of impacts if that comes  to pass that need to be thought about  and we are pretty clear that there are  workarounds for most of them to make  sure that we can be in as good a  position as we are now so you're ready  to provide more police officers for you  know when it talks about consumer panic  and increased risk of people smuggling  well I don't think these things are  necessarily going to come to pass but  it's certainly the case that these are  real risks though but the police are  need to be ready in all eventualities  for risk I mean you know policing is  about handling risk as you may remember  I spent four years as deputy mayor for  policing in London and one of the things  that time that taught me is that you  need to be ready for the unexpected and  so making sure that the police force in  its widest sense across the whole of the  country is ready for any eventuality  whether it's brexit related or otherwise  will be a key part of the strategy and  if talks in the deep Boris Johnson talks  about having more police officers and  20,000 extra officers yes the big  promise how much does that going to cost  so the first year we've scoped out looks  about 500 million we reckon about 1.1  billion over the whole period but what  the prime minister said quite clearly is  that 20,000 is the number we need to  focus on now I'm in conversation with  the wider policing family about the  practicalities and logistics of  delivering those 20,000 over the next 36  months and whatever costs fall out of  that obviously we'll have to have  discussions with the Treasury so it  could be even it could be even more than  1.1 billion well we'll see let's see  where we get to because there's lots of  other aspects to be looked at I mean one  of the pleasing things that the Prime  Minister is kicking off this weekend as  well is for us to realise that this is a  system that we're dealing with it's not  just about policing that we also have to  look at the criminal justice system the  prison system offender management the  courts the Crown Prosecution Service  that all of this fits together as a  system for managing crime and hopefully  pushing it downwards as far as we  possibly can and so he's making some  announcements today about you know more  funding going into the prison system  that will have more prison places to  provide for more violent offenders to  spend more time inside then tomorrow he  saw  a roundtable on violence specifically  with you know members of the criminal  justice system coming together with the  policing community to talk about what we  could do to drive violence down in the  country but have you have you worked out  presumably you have at this stage where  those extra police officers are actually  going to go I mean where they most need  it right now no so that's one of the  conversations that we'll be having with  police and not so noisy so the stage  looks like this so we're at about a  hundred and twenty three thousand police  officers at the moment there was a plan  already afoot from a billion pounds of  extra funding last year to recruit  another three and a half thousand across  the country we're now putting in place  to launch in September the next wave to  get the twenty thousand on top of that  over the next thirty six months between  then and now we have to have a  conversation about the deployment of  those police officers and critically  what they're going to be doing because  what the public want to see is a  restored sense of governance of the  public realm right alongside some of the  enhancements to stop and search so we're  putting in place today to deal with the  big rise in knife crime that we really  want to address assertively we do need  to address this general sense among the  public that they want more reassurance  from that police officers and do you  kind of concede that you know extra  police officers is is certainly a good  thing and many people within the police  and elsewhere will be relieved to see  that although first off it may not quite  even get us back to the peak in 2010 I  mean we're not we're not necessarily  even going to hit that peak pretty  austere attea are we what we need to get  the numbers back up because what we want  to do is both increase the capacity and  the effectiveness of the police now I've  got a 36-month job to get these 20,000  that's a big enough job on its own it'll  be the biggest expansion in policing  probably ever and so once we get to that  stage let's see where we are the  critical thing though is not so much to  focus on the number of police officers  but on the product on what are the crime  figures doing and you know we have seen  unfortunately over the last couple of  years an alarming rise for example in  serious youth violence which mirrors  arise we saw back in 2006 seven and then  in eight that we need to get on top of  an arrest and that will be the primary  focus you know we can get obsessed as we  obviously do in government on inputs but  really we need to focus on outputs and  what the public wants is to see a  reduction in crime generally but and  again police officers is one  but are you you know are you going to  have a place for them to actually work  out of I mean there are big concerns  about many police stations have been  shut down about 600 over the last few  years so are you going to start actually  buying them back you're going to start  building new police stations well we do  need to talk about logistics I mean when  we first announced the 20,000 police  officers there was some hilarity in the  media because I referred to one of the  restraints being access to lockers you  know police officers carry a lot of  equipment and so 20,000 lockers is a lot  of space pleasingly I've already had  contact some local government colleagues  counselors and others saying that they  would be happy to work with us looking  for the space that we need for police  officers and that certainly I think an  opportunity there for us to look across  the whole of the estate of the public  sector local government you know  certainly in some parts the country the  fire service and the police I was  already working together to see what we  can do to embed police officers as part  of that wider family and it speaks as I  say to a general systemic approach to  crime that we need to take account of  now you know we can't arrest our way  necessarily out of these problems we all  need to work together on the system  alongside as I say more assertive  enforcement you can't arrest your way  out but your boss Priti Patel talks  about people needing to fill terror  towards the police do you think that's a  fair assessment well we certainly want  criminals to be in fear of the police  detecting them and catching them and  putting them inside yes I mean you know  we know that the greatest deterrent to  crime is the perception of the  likelihood of getting caught and that's  psychologically what we need to increase  I mean one of the advantages for example  of section 60 stop and search which the  Home Secretary has announced she's  expanding powers of today is that it  speaks with kind of high-profile police  presence in a particular geography that  raises the perception of the likelihood  of detection of a knife and that means  that you're going to diminish the  likelihood that somebody is going to  carry one in the first place and  prevention is really what we're after  but there are also other concerns with  stop and search about potentially  increasing racial tension as well I mean  proportionally by far in a way the the  biggest proportion of people who are  stopped and searched are black young men  for the most part yeah look it the same  was true back in 2008 when we instituted  a big push against knife crime in London  and it's definitely the case that you  know we have to work  very closely with all communities across  the country to make sure that stop and  search is done in a sensitive and  professional way now things have changed  you know the vast majority of uniformed  officers now wear a body camera so the  interaction between them and the person  who's being searched can be filmed which  will lead to you know a  professionalization and in fact a better  reaction with the person's being  searched as well but you know in many  parts of the country there are people  stepping forward from all sorts of  communities particularly parents saying  they are so concerned about the welfare  of their young people that they  recognize that while stop and search is  controversial and can be assertive  you know it's necessary when you're  facing a crisis like this very quick  question because we're almost at the end  once the next election gonna be oh I  have absolutely no idea I hope it won't  be until the end of the the five year  term which is 2022 okay but who knows  okay you hope so okay well Kip Morehouse  we'll see whether there will indeed be  that election sooner or later

